Problem Statement: to find the length of the longest sequence of consecutive integers present in an unsorted array.

1. Brute Force Approach:
In the brute force approach, we consider every element of the array as a potential starting point of a consecutive sequence.
Steps:
1. Traverse the array and select each element as the starting element of a sequence.
2. Store the selected element in a variable called current.
3. Check whether current + 1 exists in the array using linear search.
4. If it exists, increment current and increase the sequence length.
5. Repeat this process until the next consecutive element is not found.
6. Update the maximum sequence length whenever a longer sequence is found.
7. Continue until every element has been considered.
8. Return the maximum length.

Code:

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& nums) {
    int n = nums.size();
    int maxi = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int current = nums[i];
        int count = 1;

        while (find(nums.begin(), nums.end(),
                    current + 1) != nums.end()) {
            current++;
            count++;
        }

        maxi = max(maxi, count);
    }

    return maxi;
}

int main() {
    vector<int> nums = {100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2};

    cout << longestConsecutive(nums);

    return 0;
}

Time Complexity: O(N^3)
Space Complexity: O(1)
